(2393067, 327768, 37893, '<td valign="top">Hey Scott-<br/> <br/>I think this was a missed opportunity. Night photography almost always has that "magic hour" which is typically an hour just before or after sunset. I think you just missed it. The foreground and background is black with hardly any definition to them. A silhouette almost always never scores high here for a night shot unless there are other things in the composition that leads the eye throughout the photo. The sky is too flat with no visual interest. Maybe if there was more ambient lighting this would have done better (more cars on the road, street lamps, etc).<br/> <br/>It''s a good shot technicaly except for the sensor dust or maybe filter dust spots that are faintly seen in the upper left hand of the skies. Don''t fret dude. I would reshoot this type of image and post it for critique sometime ;)<br/> <br/>Cheers,<br/> <br/>Rikki</td>', 'Hey Scott- I think this was a missed opportunity. Night photography almost always has that "magic hour" which is typically an hour just before or after sunset. I think you just missed it. The foreground and background is black with hardly any definition to them. A silhouette almost always never scores high here for a night shot unless there are other things in the composition that leads the eye throughout the photo. The sky is too flat with no visual interest. Maybe if there was more ambient lighting this would have done better (more cars on the road, street lamps, etc). It''s a good shot technicaly except for the sensor dust or maybe filter dust spots that are faintly seen in the upper left hand of the skies. Don''t fret dude. I would reshoot this type of image and post it for critique sometime ;) Cheers, Rikki', '2006-05-08 11:51:16.000000', NULL, 0),
